const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q: "What is a BMR calculator?", a: "A BMR calculator estimates how many calories your body burns at complete rest for basic functions. It uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation with your age, sex, height, and weight." },
  { q: "What is my BMR?", a: "Your BMR varies based on your stats. Use our <a href=\"/bmr-calculator/\">BMR calculator</a> to get your exact number. A typical range: 1,200–2,000 calories/day depending on size and sex." },
  { q: "How is BMR calculated?", a: "Using Mifflin-St Jeor: Men BMR=10×weight+6.25×height-5×age+5; Women BMR=10×weight+6.25×height-5×age-161. cm and kg required." },
  { q: "How does BMR affect weight loss?", a: "Your BMR sets the floor for your metabolism. Weight loss calculators use it to find maintenance calories, then subtract a deficit. Lower BMR means lower potential intake." },
  { q: "How do I use BMR results for meal planning?", a: "Start with your BMR, multiply by your activity factor to get TDEE, then adjust for your goal. Use the <a href=\"/macro-calculator/\">macro calculator</a> to split your calories into protein, carbs, and fats for each meal." },
  { q: "Should I recalculate my BMR after weight loss?", a: "Yes. As you lose weight, your BMR decreases because there is less body mass to maintain. Recalculate every 4–6 weeks or after a 5 kg change to keep your targets accurate." },
  { q: "Is BMR the same as the calories shown on food labels?", a: "No. Food labels show the energy content of food (kilojoules or calories). Your BMR is how many calories your body burns at rest. The two numbers are not directly comparable — your BMR is a measure of expenditure, while food labels measure intake." },
  { q: "Can I use BMR to set my protein target?", a: "BMR alone is not enough to set protein targets. Use the <a href=\"/protein-calculator/\">protein calculator</a> for a personalized protein goal based on your weight and activity level." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="BMR Calculator — Find Your Basal Metabolic Rate"
  h1="BMR Calculator"
  description="Free BMR calculator using Mifflin-St Jeor equation. Find calories burned at rest. Includes BMR meaning, calculation, and uses for weight goals."
  path="/guides/bmr-calculator/"
  category="Guides"
  categoryPath="/guides/"
  lead="Your BMR is the calories you burn at rest. Calculate it for weight loss or muscle gain."
  updated="July 2026"
  faqs={faqs}
>

  <MedicalNote />

  <p><strong>BMR calculator</strong> results tell you how many calories your body needs at complete rest. This is the foundation for any nutrition plan. Whether you want to lose fat, build muscle, or maintain your current weight, your BMR is the number you start from.</p>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/resting-flame.svg" alt="Illustration of resting metabolism showing calories burned at complete rest." />

  <h2>What BMR means for your metabolism</h2>
  <p>BMR accounts for 60–70% of your daily calories burned. The remaining calories come from digesting food (~10%) and movement (~20–30%). Understanding this breakdown helps you see why your BMR is so important — it is the engine that drives your daily energy needs, even when you are not exercising.</p>
  <p>When you know your BMR, you can see how much of your daily calorie burn is automatic and how much is under your control through activity and exercise. This insight makes it easier to set realistic calorie targets and understand why weight loss is not always as simple as "eat less."</p>

  <h2>BMR calculator formula — Mifflin-St Jeor</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Sex</th><th>BMR Formula</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) - 5 × age + 5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) - 5 × age - 161</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Use our <a href="/bmr-calculator/">BMR calculator</a> for automatic calculation. The formula requires metric units — convert imperial measurements before using it manually.</p>

  <h2>Average BMR by demographics</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Profile</th><th>Typical BMR range</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Woman: 30, 65 kg, 165 cm</td><td>~1,300–1,500 calories</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Man: 30, 80 kg, 180 cm</td><td>~1,700–1,900 calories</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Woman: 50, 75 kg</td><td>~1,300–1,500 calories</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Man: 50, 85 kg</td><td>~1,600–1,800 calories</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Factors that raise BMR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Muscle mass:</strong> Each kg of muscle adds 15–25 calories/day at rest</li>
    <li><strong>Youth:</strong> Metabolism slows ~2–5% per decade after 25</li>
    <li><strong>Height:</strong> Larger bodies burn more calories at rest</li>
    <li><strong>Organ size:</strong> Some organs burn more calories than others</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Using BMR for weight loss</h2>
  <p>To lose weight:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Calculate BMR with our calculator</li>
    <li>Multiply by activity factor to get TDEE (<a href="/guides/maintenance-calorie-calculator/">maintenance calories</a>)</li>
    <li>Subtract 250–500 calories for weight loss</li>
    <li>Never go below 1,200 calories (women) or 1,500 (men)</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>BMR vs TDEE vs RMR comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>BMR</td><td>Basal Metabolic Rate</td><td>Calories at complete rest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TDEE</td><td>Total Daily Energy Expenditure</td><td>Full day's calories burned</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RMR</td><td>Resting Metabolic Rate</td><td>Similar to BMR, easier to measure</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>BMR vs TDEE — a deeper look</h2>
  <p>BMR and TDEE are related but measure different things. BMR is your resting metabolic rate — the calories your body burns to keep you alive. TDEE is the total number of calories you burn in a day, including BMR plus all activity.</p>
  <p>The relationship is straightforward: TDEE = BMR × activity factor. The activity factor depends on how much you move:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Activity Level</th><th>Multiplier</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sedentary</td><td>1.2</td><td>Desk job, little or no exercise</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lightly active</td><td>1.375</td><td>Light exercise 1–3 days/week</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Moderately active</td><td>1.55</td><td>Moderate exercise 3–5 days/week</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Very active</td><td>1.725</td><td>Hard exercise 6–7 days/week</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Extra active</td><td>1.9</td><td>Very hard exercise, physical job</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Use the <a href="/tdee-calculator/">TDEE calculator</a> to find your exact number, or the <a href="/maintenance-calories-calculator/">maintenance calories calculator</a> for a simpler tool.</p>

  <h2>How BMR changes with weight loss</h2>
  <p>As you lose weight, your BMR decreases. This happens for two reasons: you have less body mass to maintain, and the body adapts to conserve energy — a phenomenon called adaptive thermogenesis. This is why weight loss often slows down over time, even when you stick to the same calorie intake.</p>
  <p>To account for this, recalculate your BMR and TDEE every 4–6 weeks or after a 5 kg weight change. The <a href="/weight-loss-calculator/">weight loss calculator</a> can help you plan for these metabolic adaptations and adjust your targets accordingly.</p>

  <h2>Best practices for using BMR in planning</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Always use your BMR as a starting point, not a final answer.</strong> Your BMR tells you the minimum your body needs at rest. Your actual needs are higher once you account for daily activity.</li>
    <li><strong>Be honest about your activity level.</strong> Overestimating activity is the most common mistake when estimating TDEE. If you sit at a desk all day and do not exercise, you are sedentary — not lightly active.</li>
    <li><strong>Recalculate regularly.</strong> Your body composition changes over time, and so does your BMR. Update your numbers every 4–6 weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Combine BMR with macro targets.</strong> Use the <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a> to turn your calorie targets into protein, carb, and fat goals for each meal.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not drop below your BMR.</strong> Eating below your BMR for extended periods can cause muscle loss, hormonal disruption, and metabolic slowdown. Use a moderate deficit instead.</li>
  </ol>

